<item>
            <title>[LU-20759] mgs: return -EINVAL on bad param in mgs_modify_param()</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20759</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;mgs_modify_param() at lustre/mgs/mgs_llog.c:1316 returns positive EINVAL (+22) instead of -EINVAL when a non-delete set arrives with no parsable value. The caller in mgs_wlp_lcfg() checks rc &amp;lt; 0, so the positive return is treated as success and falls through to write a new llog record after the cancel-old-entry step bailed early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Reachability:&lt;/b&gt; This path is currently unreachable through the normal mgs_set_param() ioctl flow because mgs_param_empty() upstream maps the trigger conditions (no =, or = with empty RHS) to del=true, which sends control into the MPM_DELETE branch instead. So no user-visible bug today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why fix it anyway:&lt;/b&gt; The non-reachability is a non-local invariant that depends on every caller of mgs_modify_param() doing the right empty-value mapping first. Totality over defaults &#8212; the function should reject bad input on its own terms. If a future caller invokes mgs_modify_param() directly with del=false and a value-less param, the sign error becomes a real silent-write bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; One-character change &#8212; rc = EINVAL &#8594; rc = -EINVAL.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
